  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\n This approach leverages the symmetry of the Erds minimum overlap problem to reduce dimensionality. The key insight is that an optimal solution likely exhibits symmetry under reflection, allowing us to represent the 70-dimensional problem using only 35 variables. The optimization is performed using a combination of stochastic gradient descent (SGD) and analytical gradients to balance exploration and exploitation. The work focuses on the top 15 lags due to their dominant effect on the objective value.\n\n Mathematical specification\n We represent the function h using its first 35 points, with the remaining 35 points defined by h(x+1) = 1 - h(x). This symmetry ensures the integral condition and domain periodicity. The objective function is defined as C5 = dx * max_k correl(h, 1-h, mode='full'), where dx = 2/70.\n\n Implementation\n 1. Parent validation: Extract the first 35 points from initial_h_values\n 2. Multi-start from provided parent and random initializations\n 3. SGD with adaptive learning rate and momentum\n 4. Analytical gradient computation for top 15 lags\n 5. Repair mechanism: Coastal drift and mirror symmetry\n 6. Final polishing with SLSQP\n\n Search choices:\n - Top 15 lags selected by magnitude\n - Maximum 1000 SGD steps\n - Learning rate: 0.01 to 0.0001 during parameter annealing\n\n Budget and robustness:\n - 5000 evaluations of objective function\n - Repair fallback on timeout\n - Automatic detection of convergence stagnation\n\n Validation: Clip values, enforce integral, reevaluate all lags",
